ANSIBLE PLAYBOOK--------------

Ansible Playbook is a YAML file that contains a list of tasks which will run automatically on remote servers.
It is used to automate configuration and management of multiple servers.

In simple words:
Playbook = file that contains automation instructions

Playbooks usually have the extension .yml or .yaml

Example

playbook.yml
Why we use Playbooks

Reduce manual work

Run the same task on multiple servers

Easy automation

Faster server configuration

Useful for DevOps automation

Example:
If you want to install nginx on 20 servers, you can do it with one playbook command.

Basic Structure of an Ansible Playbook

A playbook mainly contains these parts:

Hosts

Tasks

Modules

Variables

Handlers

1. Hosts

Hosts define which servers the playbook will run on.

Example

hosts: webservers

or

hosts: all

all means the playbook will run on all servers in the inventory.

2. Tasks

Tasks are the actions that Ansible performs on servers.

Examples of tasks:

install packages

start services

copy files

create users

Example task

tasks:
  - name: install nginx
    apt:
      name: nginx
      state: present

This task installs nginx.

3. Modules

Modules are pre-built tools in Ansible used to perform specific tasks.

Examples

apt → install packages on Ubuntu/Debian

yum → install packages on RedHat

service → start or stop services

copy → copy files to servers

file → manage file permissions

Example

apt:
  name: nginx
  state: present
4. Variables

Variables store values that can be used multiple times in a playbook.

Example

vars:
  package_name: nginx

Using variable in task

apt:
  name: "{{ package_name }}"
  state: present

Variables make playbooks more flexible and reusable.

5. Handlers

Handlers are special tasks that run only when a change happens.

They are mostly used to restart services.

Example

handlers:
  - name: restart nginx
    service:
      name: nginx
      state: restarted
Basic Playbook Example
---
- name: install nginx on servers
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: install nginx
      apt:
        name: nginx
        state: present

    - name: start nginx service
      service:
        name: nginx
        state: started

Explanation

hosts → servers where playbook will run
become → use sudo privileges
tasks → list of actions to perform

How to Run a Playbook

Command

ansible-playbook playbook.yml

Example

ansible-playbook nginx.yml

This command will execute the playbook on all target servers.

Important Points

Playbooks are written in YAML format

Indentation (spaces) is very important

Playbooks use modules to perform tasks

A playbook can contain multiple tasks

Used for automation and configuration management

Simple Real Example

Suppose you want to:

Install nginx

Start nginx service

Copy configuration file

on many servers.

Instead of doing it manually on each server, you can create one playbook and run one command.

Ansible will automatically complete all tasks on all servers.